  "summary": "US Ambassador Ronald Johnson said new security agreements will let US government activities resume immediately in phases, restarting avocado operations in Michoacán. Separately, Mexico's Treasury issued modified anti-money-laundering rules, with phased implementation beginning in November 2026.",
  "lead": {
    "h1": "US ambassador says security agreements will resume avocado operations in Michoacán",
    "context": "Ronald Johnson said the commitments would strengthen security and allow US government activities to resume immediately in phases.",
    "background": "Michoacán is Mexico's main avocado export state, and US Department of Agriculture inspectors normally certify orchards there before fruit can cross the border, which is why a security-related suspension halts the trade until Washington judges it safe to send staff back. Avocados sit within the broader US-Mexico goods relationship where about 80% of Mexico's exports go to the United States.",
    "view": "This is a security concession, not a trade agreement, and it addresses federal-state coordination rather than the extortion and cargo theft that Mexico's statistics agency data shows go unreported 93.2% of the time and fall hardest on small producers who cannot pay for protection. The government's claim of a 46% drop in daily homicides since September 2024 is still preliminary prosecutor data, so a security commitment announced this week rests on figures that could be revised once final death certificates arrive.",
    "prediction": "US personnel most likely resume avocado-related activity in Michoacán in stages over the coming weeks, with no fixed date given. That would be disproven if Washington re-suspends operations following a new violence alert, which is the same trigger that halted the activity this time.",
