      "headline": "Mexico's Treasury announces new anti-money-laundering rules effective November 2026",
      "context": "Mexico's Secretaría de Hacienda has issued modified rules on anti-money-laundering requirements with phased implementation beginning in November 2026.",
      "background": "The reform amends Mexico's anti-money-laundering law (LFPIORPI), which governs the roughly 120,000 registered individuals and businesses in \"vulnerable activities\" such as real estate, jewelry and financial services that must report suspicious transactions.",
      "view": "The shift from a uniform compliance model to a risk-based one lands with a long runway: rules take effect November 30, 2026, main requirements start in 2027, and audits begin only in 2028. That gap matters because Mexico's commercial-bank credit to the private sector is just 35.5% of gross domestic product, so tighter money-laundering oversight targets the formal financial system while informal channels, where 55.2% of workers already operate, remain largely untouched.",
      "prediction": "Most likely outcome: large firms and financial institutions begin building risk-classification systems well before the 2027 deadline, while smaller registered businesses delay compliance until closer to the 2028 audit start. This would be wrong if Hacienda enforces the November 2026 date more strictly than stated or moves audits earlier.",
